{
  "answer": "Clifford's essay defends an ethics of belief centered on the duty of inquiry. The core principle is that it is wrong always, everywhere, and for any one to believe anything upon insufficient evidence. Belief is not a private whim; it guides action and is socially inherited, so it must be held to evidential standards. A belief is justified only when it has been honestly earned by patient investigation rather than produced by stifling doubt. Sincere conviction is not an excuse: the shipowner may sincerely believe in the soundness of his vessel, but he had no right to believe on such evidence. Outcomes are also irrelevant to culpability; if the ship arrives safely, the owner is not innocent but merely not found out, because right and wrong concern the origin of belief, not its truth or fruits. Clifford further argues that belief and action cannot be cleanly separated: a belief not founded on fair inquiry unfits a person for necessary inquiry, and no real belief is wholly insignificant. Testimony can justify belief only if the hearer has reasonable grounds to trust the informant's veracity, knowledge, and judgment; merely many people repeating a claim does not help unless at least one person can know and speak truth. Inference beyond experience is legitimate only on the assumption of uniformity: what we do not know is like what we know, and such inference is right when it is practically demonstrative. Throughout, the shipowner, island agitators, Mohammed/Buddha, chemist, and explorers are illustrative or conditional alternatives. Clifford presents them as supposed cases or hypothetical witness types, not as verified assertions about actual people. The Mohammed/Buddha discussion is explicitly conditional: even granting their claimed characters, the question remains whether they could know what they claim. Thus these examples should not be read as historical facts.\n\nObjection: Clifford's principle overreaches. From the premise that every belief exerts social influence, he concludes that any insufficiently evidenced belief is a sin. But that conclusion needs an additional premise that the believer is voluntarily responsible for the insufficiency. If a person is non-culpably ignorant or has no realistic opportunity to investigate, their belief may still be socially risky, but it is not clearly blameworthy. (This added premise is mine, not Clifford's.) Reply: Clifford can accept this nuance without giving up the principle. His central target is the voluntary suppression of doubt and refusal to inquire; he says it is never lawful to stifle a doubt and that the busy man who lacks time to study should have no time to believe. The inculcated child is not the primary object of blame; the teachers who made Israel sin are. So the objection weakens the 'always' rhetoric but leaves intact the requirement to proportion belief to evidence where inquiry is possible.",
