  "answer": "Clifford treats belief as a morally evaluable act because every real belief shapes action and character, and no belief is private. His central norm is: 'it is wrong always, everywhere and for any one, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence.' A belief is justified only when it has been honestly earned by patient investigation and is proportioned to the evidence before the believer. A belief is blameworthy when it is acquired or kept by suppressing doubts, avoiding inquiry, or resting on insufficient evidence. The shipowner case makes this clear: even a sincere conviction cannot help if he 'had no right to believe on such evidence as was before him.' Clifford says he acquired belief 'not by honestly earning it in patient investigation, but by stifling his doubts.' The island agitators likewise had sincere but stolen convictions.\n\nEvidence, sincere conviction, outcomes: For Clifford, the relevant question is not truth/falsity or good/bad consequences but the origin of the belief in relation to evidence. When the ship is supposed safe, guilt is 'not one jot' less; right and wrong are settled at the time of action and no accidental failure of fruits can change them. The rule is 'not what it was, but how he got it.' Sincerity is irrelevant because one can work oneself into a comfortable conviction; outcomes are irrelevant because they do not repair faulty epistemic origin. The social dimension matters: no belief is truly insignificant; it prepares us to receive more of its like and may later explode into overt action. Therefore the duty to question extends to all persons and all beliefs.\n\nTestimony: Clifford allows belief on testimony only when one has reasonable grounds for trusting the speaker's veracity, knowledge, and judgment. A good moral character is not enough; the Muslim appeal to Mohammed's noble character is rejected because it shows at most honesty, not knowledge of supernatural matters. The chemist example shows authority is acceptable when the claim is within human verification and the informant has appropriate expertise; the Arctic explorer's observed report is acceptable, but the old whaler's unsupported extrapolation about ice to the Pole is not. Tradition is subject to the same test: it is not a set of propositions to accept but a store of questions, concepts, and methods to be tested.\n\nInference beyond experience: For Clifford, every belief as guide to action goes beyond present experience. The burnt child assumes the future fire is like the past one. Therefore the question is not whether we may go beyond experience, but how far and in what manner. The rule is uniformity: 'We may believe what goes beyond our experience, only when it is inferred from that experience by the assumption that what we do not know is like what we know.' This allows spectroscopic inference to hydrogen in the sun if the method is checked, and historical inference under a uniformity of human character. But no evidence can justify a belief contrary to or outside the uniformity of nature; within the range of human action and verification we may form actual beliefs, beyond it only hypotheses for asking questions.\n\nExamples vs. actual people: The shipowner and island accusers are presented as 'supposed cases' and their outcome variations are explicitly conditional ('Let us alter the case...'). They illustrate principles rather than report named actual persons. The passages about Mohammed and Buddha discuss reported claims and testimonial authority; the essay does not assert as fact what those figures privately knew. It says about Mohammed's possible private state only that 'It is most probable that he himself never doubted of the matter, or thought of asking the question'; this is a probabilistic comment for the argument, not a biographical assertion. I therefore do not import external biographical facts.\n\nObjection: The universal duty may be too demanding. Ordinary busy people cannot investigate every belief, and a reasonable division of cognitive labor seems to require accepting expert testimony even when one lacks the time or competence to assess the evidence. This objection's premise is my own/objector's, not Clifford's: a moral rule that cannot be followed by ordinary agents risks demanding an impossible suspension of belief.\n\nReply: Clifford's own premises provide an answer. He does not require personal verification of every matter; his testimony rule permits accepting a chemist's result when there are reasonable grounds for veracity, knowledge, and judgment and the claim is within human verifiability. He also allows action on probabilities without full belief where evidence is not yet sufficient: 'there are many cases in which it is our duty to act upon probabilities.' Thus the objection overstates paralysis. However, the residual tension is real: if a busy person cannot assess even the testimonial grounds, Clifford says he should have no time to believe. That may be harsh, but it follows from his prioritization of epistemic responsibility over comfort.",
